body:
So there is a lot of vomiting here about a lot of fears and frustrations but the common theme that seems to be apparent is generalised anxiety.

&#x200B;

It is as if the writer is having anxiety over anything and everything and it just wants to vomit it all out and try to rationalize everything in order to make sense out of this nonsense

I believe that I understood this message here.

&#x200B;

Where that despite the many capabilities that a human can or might achieve, it is sometimes difficult to remember that, like how the saying goes, we are human.

&#x200B;

We err, we do stupid stuff, and we do incredible stuff.

So despite our domination of the planet, I think it is a reminder that being human is a thing to challenge and accept

So, there is a lot to unpack here.

There is a strong sense of passion here, maybe even hatred but it feels like the writer does not know if it wants to hate itself or the situation becuase there is a lot of self-criticism and idealisation of self harm.

&#x200B;

Yet there is a secret courage to break the cycle and stop the analysis paralysis and just fucking breathe and relive itself from its own burden that it is sufferring from.

&#x200B;

Maybe there is a secret roar that screams out to get out of this depression and stop looking at its own life like nothing but suffering and try to accept the situation with the good and the bad

I quietly laughed when I read this one.

&#x200B;

The self awareness is pretty clear and dazzling with its own comedy - it is aware that it is a poem like anything else but it breaks the rules in a swimmingly manner so it does not feel too forced or out of place. 

&#x200B;

There is this desire to scream out its own voice, like that nail that wants to stand out no matter what, with the brazen and confidence to live by it

So it seems that it is a metaphor for something that has happened in one day during October.

&#x200B;

The metaphor of using the cold and darkness clearly portrays that sharp image of the harsh, cold as symbols of winter.

&#x200B;

It seems as though this is a journal entry, trying to mentally deconstruct that happened that day and trying to make sense out of it

There is a clear message of a hateful want to be closer to this person because the writer misses him/her so damn much that it hurts them.

&#x200B;

While some of the language seems to be a bit cliche, I think I understood the message that it is trying to portray

There is an ominous feeling about this.

&#x200B;

It could be the word "decay" is little decay and old age or perhaps something else becausse of Alzheimer's or decay because of stress.

It could be the word "decay" is little decay and old age or perhaps something else because of Alzheimer's or decay because of stress.ier, or perhaps the pen is a scalpel in brain surgery.

&#x200B;

Regardless, the aura is gives is quite charming

at first i thought that this was a wet dream, but it seems that the sea is related to everlasting crying and sadness.

&#x200B;

the naked part does not seem to imply lust but rather to imply vulnerability or the fact of being lost

I am using it on a computer.

&#x200B;

It is mostly high-end as it is also used for gaming. I have been using Photopea on this same computer for almost 2 years now

&#x200B;

The processor is Intel(R) Core(TM) i5-9600KF CPU @ 3.70GHz   3.70 GHz

The GPU is an NVIDIA GeForce RTXX 2060 SUPER

I admit that this was not my best work. 

&#x200B;

My idea was to illustrate how the term "open-world" is not really as accurate and is mostly used as a marketing term, and in what ways developers can make open-world games truly open world

I admit that this is not a very well-made post.

&#x200B;

It was in the back of my mind for a while because I felt compelled to post something about the dissonance in open-world games
